# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — removed entirely in .NET 5+
2. **`ThreadAbortException` will never be raised** — catch blocks for it are dead code
3. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` also throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`**

## Why Thread.Abort Was Removed

It was fundamentally unsafe — it could interrupt code mid-execution (even inside `finally` blocks in some cases), corrupt state, and cause resource leaks. .NET 8 uses **cooperative cancellation** instead.

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass token so blocking calls can be interrupted cooperatively
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — roll back partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        timeout ??= TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);

        // Signal cooperative cancellation
        await _cts.CancelAsync(); // .NET 8 addition — thread-safe cancel

        // Wait with a hard timeout
        if (await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(timeout.Value)) != _workerTask)
        {
            // Task didn't finish in time — log warning; it will still wind down
            // but we stop waiting. Do NOT try to forcibly kill it.
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Migration Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **Blocking calls ignore cancellation** | If `GetNextItem()` blocks on I/O or a `Monitor.Wait`, cancellation won't interrupt it. You **must** convert to async APIs that accept `CancellationToken`. |
| **`Thread.Sleep` → `await Task.Delay`** | `Thread.Sleep` blocks the thread pool thread and ignores cancellation. |
| **`volatile bool` flag is insufficient** | It can't wake a blocked call. `CancellationToken` integrates with `WaitHandle`, semaphores, channels, and async I/O. |
| **No forced termination exists** | If code truly hangs (e.g., unmanaged call), you must isolate it in a separate **process** (not thread) and kill the process. |
| **`CancellationTokenSource` must be disposed** | Failure to dispose leaks timer handles if `CancelAfter` is used. |
| **Don't swallow all `OperationCanceledException`** | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` guard to avoid hiding bugs. |

## If You Can't Go Fully Async

For sync-only code paths, use a dedicated thread with cooperative cancellation:

```csharp
private void ProcessLoopSync(CancellationToken token)
{
    while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        // For blocking collections, use TryTake with timeout
        if (_queue.TryTake(out var item, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1), token))
        {
            ProcessItem(item);
        }
    }
}
```

Use `BlockingCollection<T>.TryTake(out item, timeout, CancellationToken)` or `Channel<T>` (preferred in modern .NET) to make blocking calls cancellation-aware.

## Summary

Replace `Thread.Abort` → **`CancellationToken` cooperative cancellation**. There is no 1:1 replacement for forced thread termination — the design must change to cooperative patterns.
